{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## An adversarial review caught a REAL defect in my own fix — and my oracle was vacuous on exactly that axis\n\nChange 1398 had already drawn **LLM-Review +1** when a held-out adversarial review of the same\ndiff found a defect the +1 did not. Recording it because the near-miss is the useful part.\n\n### The defect: the alert fired on tickets where NOTHING was dropped\n\nI put the recording inside `_resolve_local_parent`, which **cannot see the remote snapshot**. So\nit fired whenever bug 8b25's guard fired — including when the tracker ALREADY held exactly the\nparent the user asked for. Verified at runtime:\n\n```\nCONVERGED non-epic parent (jira already == local):\n  changed = {}\n  dropped = [('RBJ-9', 'parent')]   <- SPURIOUS\n```\n\nThat is not an edge case, it is **the common shape**: a Jira sub-task's parent genuinely IS a\nnon-epic (a Story or Task), the inbound pass mirrors it into local `parent_id`, and every such\nticket then files an `outbound-field-dropped:<KEY>:parent` alert on every pass, re-filed each\ntime the 24h dedup window rolls. It **inverts the alert's meaning** — the channel would most\noften be saying \"nothing was dropped\" — which is worse than the silence this ticket set out to\nfix, because a channel that cries wolf is one operators learn to close. It also broke parity with\nthe `issuetype` sink I cited as precedent, which records only when the local value ACTUALLY\nDIFFERS from the remote.\n\n### Why my mutation checks missed it — the honest part\n\nMy `_remote()` fixture **hardcoded `remote_parent_id: None`**. A fixture pinned that way can only\never describe a tracker with no parent, so no test in the file could distinguish \"dropped\" from\n\"already there\", and all 7 cells were green against a fix that alerted on every converged\nticket. Both my mutations survived it because both perturbed the *recording*, and none perturbed\nthe *comparison that did not exist*. **A mutation check only tests the axes the fixture varies.**\n\n### The correction, under the strict order again\n\nFix reverted -> converged case added (RED, on that case alone) -> fix corrected -> GREEN. The\nrecording moved OUT of `_resolve_local_parent` into `diff_canonical_fields`, where\n`canonical_remote` is in scope; `_resolve_local_parent` now reports the suppressed key via\n`suppressed_out` and the caller records only when it DIFFERS from `remote_parent_id`. The\n2-tuple return is still intact. Three mutations now each kill a different cell:\n\n| mutation | cell that fails |\n|---|---|\n| never report a suppression (the original root cause) | 3 cells incl. the alert-store hop |\n| drop the bound check | the unbound negative control |\n| **drop the convergence gate (this defect)** | the new converged case |\n\n### Four more findings, all fixed\n\n1. **The outbound cell's \"nothing was written\" oracle could NEVER fail.** The child is a standard\n   issue, and `set_parent` never writes `fields.parent` for a non-sub-task — it writes the Epic\n   Link. So `?fields=parent` was empty by construction whatever rebar did. Now reads\n   `parent,<epic_field>` and asserts both empty. This was a textbook vacuous green in a cell\n   whose own docstring claimed to close that hole.\n2. **The re-homed INBOUND cell would have been red for an unrelated reason — and would have\n   REVERTED the instance-side move.** `parent` is not inbound-mirrored, so a local parent is\n   resolved LOCAL-WINS, and `_OUTBOUND_TO_INBOUND_FIELD` maps `parent`->`parent_id` so an\n   outbound emission SUPPRESSES the inbound mirror. Priming the child under epic A locally and\n   moving it to epic B on the instance would have had outbound re-assert A and undo the move.\n   The old sub-task shape was **accidentally immune** because 8b25 suppressed the emit — so\n   re-homing to epics removed a protection nobody knew was load-bearing. Restructured: the child\n   now starts PARENTLESS (a standard issue can, a sub-task cannot — which is why the old cell had\n   to re-parent at all), so there is nothing to re-assert and the pass takes the documented\n   \"adopt inbound, don't clobber\" path.\n3. **The 4b9e clear cell went GREEN on a FAILED READ.** A 401/403/500 returns a dict like\n   `{\"errorMessages\": [...]}`, whose `.get(\"fields\")` is None, so `assert not after` passed.\n   Status is now asserted before the value is read. A \"must be absent\" oracle needs that; the\n   positive assertions above it did not.\n4. **Dead helpers.** `probe_subtask_parent_put` / `_editmeta_ops` lost every call site in the\n   re-home. Rather than delete them, they moved to\n   `test_a_subtask_reparent_is_REFUSED_rather_than_silently_ignored` — the cell whose SUBJECT is\n   the accept-and-ignore — so the platform behaviour keeps being re-measured every run instead of\n   trusted from the map. Also removed the asymmetric `try/except Exception` around the boundness\n   probe: the same call is unguarded eleven lines later, and its only real effect was to fall\n   back to silence, restoring the exact bug being fixed.\n\nA comment-hygiene guard also failed on a raw run-id citation; the docstrings now carry full\ngrouped-hex ticket ids alongside it. That guard is in the marker-selected suite, which is why\nrunning CI's actual selection rather than a path subset mattered."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785889090334288001,"uuid":"14bd82a2-bb3a-4bac-9bdd-7d854addbd45"}